<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#biblical-hebrew">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biblical Hebrew</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Classical Hebrew</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, also called Classical Hebrew, is an archaic form of the Hebrew language, of the Canaanitic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, spoken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> in the Land of Israel. It is attested in inscriptions from about the 10th century BCE, when it was almost identical to Phoenician, and persisted as a spoken first language through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, which ended in 70 CE, before developing into Mishnaic Hebrew. It was first written in the Paleo-Hebrew alphabet, displaced after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-aramaic">Imperial Aramaic</a> alphabet, and its vowel marks, the Tiberian vocalization, were added by the Masoretes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biblical-hebrew.jpg" alt="Biblical Hebrew" width="330" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tamar Hayardeni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiloach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Biblical Hebrew</b> had a distinctive verbal system, in which narratives were told in a form combining the conjunction “and” with a verb form, and its usual word order placed the verb before the subject.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Phoenician is a Canaanite language of the Northwest Semitic division of the Semitic family, similar to and mutually intelligible with <b>Biblical Hebrew</b> and the other languages of its subgroup.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Apparent Tamil loanwords in <b>Biblical Hebrew</b> from before 500 BCE are the oldest attestation of all.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
